12:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1a",null,{"guide":{"date":"June 27, 1864","place":"Cheatham Hill, Kennesaw Mountain, Georgia","result":"Mitchell’s brigade reached the Confederate works at the Dead Angle but was repulsed","facts":[{"label":"Attack began","value":"8 a.m."},{"label":"Mitchell’s brigade","value":"Five regiments led by the 34th Illinois skirmishers"},{"label":"Ammunition carried","value":"60 rounds per U.S. soldier"},{"label":"Confederates at the tip","value":"About 180 men of the consolidated 1st and 27th Tennessee"},{"label":"Attacking force described","value":"About 4,000 in McCook’s and Mitchell’s brigades"},{"label":"Close combat","value":"Probably about 20 minutes"}],"maps":[{"title":"Troop movements at Kennesaw Mountain","caption":"The National Park Service map shows Thomas’s main attack in blue against Hardee’s Confederate line in red. The Dead Angle was on Cheatham Hill near the center of the assault.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/orgs/1539/the-southeast-chronicles-kennesaw-mountain-national-battlefield-park.htm","image":"/images/kennesaw-troop-movements.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of troop movements at Kennesaw Mountain on June 27, 1864"},{"title":"Confederate positions across Kennesaw Mountain","caption":"This 1864 drawing places Pigeon Hill, Little and Big Kennesaw, Confederate artillery, and the U.S. position north of the heights.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660525/","image":"/images/kennesaw-battlefield-drawing.jpg","alt":"1864 drawing of Confederate artillery positions across Kennesaw Mountain"},{"title":"Cheatham Hill today","caption":"Mitchell’s and McCook’s brigades attacked the salient now preserved in the southern part of Kennesaw Mountain National Battlefield Park.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-84.626%2C33.915%2C-84.576%2C33.958&layer=mapnik&marker=33.9334%2C-84.6039"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:41","audioTime":101,"title":"The episode shifts to Mitchell’s brigade","detail":"Jefferson C. Davis sent Daniel McCook Jr. and John G. Mitchell against the Dead Angle. The previous episode covered McCook; this one follows Mitchell."},{"time":"2:25","audioTime":145,"title":"John G. Mitchell commanded the second attacking brigade","detail":"The 25-year-old Ohio lawyer had led troops at Chickamauga but had seen limited combat during the Atlanta campaign."},{"time":"3:17","audioTime":197,"title":"Mitchell formed a column of regiments","detail":"The 113th Ohio led, followed by the 121st and 98th Ohio and 78th Illinois. The 34th Illinois deployed ahead as skirmishers."},{"time":"4:04","audioTime":244,"title":"The brigade was to deploy into line at the works","detail":"Henry Banning’s 121st Ohio was to move right when the leading regiment reached the defenses. The remaining regiments would extend the line farther right."},{"time":"5:26","audioTime":326,"title":"George Maney’s brigade held the Dead Angle","detail":"Maney’s Tennessee troops occupied the tip and south face of the salient. Benjamin Cheatham ordered them to hold the works."},{"time":"6:16","audioTime":376,"title":"The attack began with artillery fire","detail":"At 8 a.m., U.S. guns opened and the 34th Illinois overran the Confederate skirmish line. The brigade moved into the stream valley below the works."},{"time":"7:39","audioTime":459,"title":"Confederate soldiers saw the column approaching","detail":"Captain W. D. Kelly of the 1st Tennessee alerted the defenders as Mitchell’s troops climbed the slope."},{"time":"8:04","audioTime":484,"title":"Abatis and enfilading artillery broke up the formation","detail":"Cut saplings disrupted the U.S. column. Eight Confederate guns south of the salient fired along the attacking line."},{"time":"9:12","audioTime":552,"title":"The leading regiments reached the works","detail":"The 34th Illinois stopped 12 to 15 yards short. Men of the 113th Ohio reached the parapet, where some were killed and others captured."},{"time":"9:51","audioTime":591,"title":"The 113th Ohio lost many officers","detail":"Lieutenant Colonel Darius B. Warner was badly wounded. Ten of the regiment’s 19 officers were killed or wounded."},{"time":"10:14","audioTime":614,"title":"The 121st Ohio moved right as ordered","detail":"Banning’s regiment cleared the 113th but suffered heavy losses from the Confederate battery."},{"time":"10:51","audioTime":651,"title":"About 180 Confederates held the tip","detail":"Maney placed the consolidated 1st and 27th Tennessee in the salient while additional regiments held the adjoining works."},{"time":"13:41","audioTime":821,"title":"Hiram Field fought from the parapet","detail":"The regimental commander fired weapons passed up from the trench until a U.S. bullet grazed his head and temporarily paralyzed his left side."},{"time":"17:08","audioTime":1028,"title":"The defenders fired rapidly at close range","detail":"Sam Watkins said he fired 120 rounds and exchanged overheated muskets for weapons taken from dead comrades."},{"time":"17:32","audioTime":1052,"title":"William Hughes was mortally wounded","detail":"Hughes grabbed the barrel of a U.S. soldier’s musket while protecting Watkins and was shot through the hand and arm."},{"time":"20:48","audioTime":1248,"title":"The 6th and 9th Tennessee reinforced the salient","detail":"They moved through the trench and added their fire as the 1st and 27th Tennessee ran low on ammunition."},{"time":"21:52","audioTime":1312,"title":"Mitchell’s assault stalled","detail":"The 98th Ohio and 78th Illinois could not complete the planned deployment. The 113th and 121st Ohio and 34th Illinois were the units that closed on the works."},{"time":"23:07","audioTime":1387,"title":"The U.S. regiments fell back to dead ground","detail":"Mitchell’s men joined McCook’s survivors behind a slight rise 40 to 100 yards from the Confederate line and dug a low parapet."},{"time":"23:39","audioTime":1419,"title":"Cheatham sent reinforcements too late to engage","detail":"Jesse Finley’s brigade ran about a mile but arrived after the U.S. attack had fallen back."},{"time":"24:14","audioTime":1454,"title":"The closest fighting lasted about 20 minutes","detail":"Participant estimates varied, but the exposed position near the parapet made a much longer assault unlikely."},{"time":"26:04","audioTime":1564,"title":"Available U.S. reserves were not committed","detail":"James D. Morgan’s brigade, Absalom Baird’s division, and nearby XX Corps divisions prepared to move but received no order to join the assault."},{"time":"28:17","audioTime":1697,"title":"Earthworks allowed a smaller force to hold","detail":"McCook and Mitchell brought about 4,000 men. One Confederate estimate placed no more than 1,000 defenders in action in the attacked sector at one time."},{"time":"29:52","audioTime":1792,"title":"The XIV Corps attack did not break the line","detail":"Mitchell’s and McCook’s brigades came closer to the works than other attacks but did not penetrate Cheatham’s position."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"George H. Thomas","command":"Army of the Cumberland","subordinates":"John M. Palmer’s XIV Corps and Joseph Hooker’s XX Corps"},{"commander":"John M. Palmer","command":"XIV Corps","subordinates":"Jefferson C. Davis’s division"},{"commander":"Jefferson C. Davis","command":"2nd Division, XIV Corps","subordinates":"Daniel McCook Jr., John G. Mitchell, and James D. Morgan"},{"commander":"John G. Mitchell","command":"2nd Brigade","subordinates":"34th Illinois; 78th Illinois; 98th, 113th, and 121st Ohio"},{"commander":"Henry B. Banning","command":"121st Ohio Infantry","subordinates":"Second regiment in Mitchell’s assault column"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"William J. Hardee","command":"Hardee’s Corps","subordinates":"Benjamin F. Cheatham’s division"},{"commander":"Benjamin F. Cheatham","command":"Cheatham’s Division","subordinates":"George Maney and Jesse Finley"},{"commander":"George Maney","command":"Maney’s Brigade","subordinates":"Held the Dead Angle"},{"commander":"Hiram S. Field","command":"Consolidated 1st and 27th Tennessee","subordinates":"About 180 men at the tip of the salient"},{"commander":"Jesse J. Finley","command":"Finley’s Brigade","subordinates":"Moved to reinforce the salient"}]}],"people":[{"name":"John G. Mitchell","role":"Led the second U.S. brigade against the Dead Angle","side":"United States"},{"name":"George Maney","role":"Commanded the Confederate brigade defending the salient","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Hiram S. Field","role":"Led the consolidated 1st and 27th Tennessee and was wounded at the parapet","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Darius B. Warner","role":"113th Ohio commander wounded while urging his regiment forward","side":"United States"},{"name":"Henry B. Banning","role":"Commanded the 121st Ohio during its move to the right of the assault column","side":"United States"},{"name":"Sam R. Watkins","role":"1st Tennessee soldier whose account describes the close fighting at the salient","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"William Hughes","role":"1st Tennessee soldier mortally wounded while protecting Watkins","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"William Hughes","side":"Confederacy","role":"Soldier, 1st Tennessee Infantry","outcome":"Mortally wounded while protecting Sam Watkins during the close fighting"},{"name":"Darius B. Warner","side":"United States","role":"Lieutenant colonel, 113th Ohio Infantry","outcome":"Badly wounded in the right arm near the Confederate works"},{"name":"Hiram S. Field","side":"Confederacy","role":"Colonel, consolidated 1st and 27th Tennessee","outcome":"Shot in the head and temporarily paralyzed; recovered"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/kennesaw-troop-movements.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map showing U.S. attacks and the Confederate line at Kennesaw Mountain","caption":"The U.S. main attack struck the Confederate line at Cheatham Hill while other forces attacked or demonstrated farther north and south.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/orgs/1539/the-southeast-chronicles-kennesaw-mountain-national-battlefield-park.htm"},{"image":"/images/kennesaw-battlefield-drawing.jpg","alt":"Theodore R. Davis drawing of Confederate artillery positions at Kennesaw Mountain","caption":"Theodore R. Davis recorded Confederate artillery positions on the Kennesaw heights during the campaign.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660525/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Episode 524 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2026/04/12/civil-war-podcast-episode-524/"},{"label":"National Park Service battle summary","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=ga015"},{"label":"National Park Service troop movements map","url":"https://www.nps.gov/orgs/1539/the-southeast-chronicles-kennesaw-mountain-national-battlefield-park.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service Union order of battle","url":"https://www.nps.gov/kemo/learn/historyculture/union-order-of-battle.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service Confederate order of battle","url":"https://www.nps.gov/kemo/learn/historyculture/confederate-order-of-battle.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress Kennesaw battlefield drawing","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660525/"}]}}]}]
